{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792310871,"nonce":"mUdk9Z4WTwflN6SCQJN94ZfyLMQ=","title":"Q8 KV on wasm RESOLVED via the COMPONENT loader (2cc6f13 find fix) — the dylink.0 side-module works; in-guest port (be3c03e) was reverted ...","message":"**Goal (user directive, architectural).** Rayzor's wasm runtime topology is **wasm-components + wasm-linker**: the host provides PRIMITIVES only (thread-spawn, I/O), NEVER computation. Capabilities like the Q8 KV cache must be portable wasm COMPONENTS loaded by the wasm-linker — not native-only dylibs (today's `libnue_plugins.dylib`) and not host-side native shims. This is what fixes the wasm 4GB hang architecturally: native stays \u003c2GB via the Q8 KV cache from the nue-plugins dylib; wasm can't load that dylib so it falls back to the F32 cache (~2GB at ctx 4096) + model (~1.9GB) → 4GB wall. The fix is to load the SAME plugin as a wasm component. Do NOT reimplement Q8 in runtime-wasm (divergent copy = also wrong). See [[perf_wasm_host_parallel_matmul]].\n\n**2026-06-15 — RESOLVED the RIGHT way (the \"blocked on collision\" note below is OUTDATED).** First I shipped an in-guest port (be3c03e) — a divergent copy that violated the directive — then REVERTED it (0c3e47f) when the user chose the component path. Turns out the component loader was NOT blocked: the dylink.0/`__memory_base`/GOT loader (`parse_side_dylink` + the side-module block in wasm_runner.rs) already handles the data-layout collision. The PIC side-module `target/wasm32-wasip1-threads-pic/release/nue_plugins.wasm` was built and loads + runs CORRECTLY (France MATCH + 600-tok coherent, no trap). The ONLY real gap: `find_wasm_side_module` only tried CWD-relative paths and `rayzor run` cd's into the project dir → never found. FIX (2cc6f13): resolve relative to the rayzor exe (`\u003croot\u003e/target/release/rayzor` → `\u003croot\u003e/target/wasm32-…/nue_plugins.wasm`). Now `RAYZOR_KV_Q8=1` auto-loads the component, no override. Perf (settled, 400 tok): F32 25 vs Q8 21 (short ctx F32 wins); at 600 tok Q8 21 vs F32 15.6 (Q8 flat, wins long) — \"parity short, win long\" + always 3.76× less KV. Loader still warns \"24/24 GOT.func unresolved (dead fn-ptrs, defaulted 0)\" — harmless in the Q8 path but worth a look. So: same nue-plugins as a wasm component, NO divergent copy, directive honored. On main.\n\n**4-step plan + status:**\n- **1/4 DONE (8c6c473):** runtime-wasm exports the 6 `rayzor_plugin_tensor_*` accessors (alloc_zeros/data/dtype/ndim/shape/is_contiguous) in `runtime-wasm/src/tensor.rs` — the composition seam the plugin resolves against. Verified exported from the .wasm.\n- **2/4 DONE (a3df89c):** `nue-plugins/.cargo/config.toml` makes `cd nue-plugins \u0026\u0026 cargo build --release --target wasm32-wasip1-threads` reproducibly emit a wasm module that EXPORTS the Q8 capability (`rayzor_kv_cache_q8_{alloc,free,append,dequant_view}`, `rayzor_tensor_flash_attn_decode_q8`, `plugin_describe`, `wasi_thread_start`) and keeps the 6 `rayzor_plugin_tensor_*` as IMPORTS via `-C link-arg=--allow-undefined` (rust-lld errors on `-Wl,` prefix — pass `--allow-undefined` bare). Target features mirror runtime-wasm (+atomics,+bulk-memory,+mutable-globals,+simd128,+relaxed-simd); on-wasm NEON paths cfg'd to aarch64 → scalar fallback.\n- **4/4 LARGELY ALREADY DONE:** the plugin manifest already flows into the wasm path. `prepare_wasm_compile_inputs` (src/wasm_cmd.rs:111-128) dlopens the native dylib + pushes it as a compiler plugin BEFORE MIR lowering, so `mir_result.qualified_method_map` carries `nue.transformer.KvCacheQ8.alloc → rayzor_kv_cache_q8_alloc`; `WasmBackend::compile_with_method_map` turns that into the guest's import NAME. So the guest already emits `rayzor_kv_cache_q8_*` imports matching the plugin's exports. (The `let _loaded_native_libs = ...` discard at line ~291 only drops the dylib HANDLE, which wasm doesn't need.) Caveat: still requires the native dylib present to read the manifest.\n- **3/4 BLOCKED — the real wall:** the 3-way merge (user + runtime-wasm + plugin) in `compiler/src/codegen/wasm_linker.rs` (2580 lines, hand-rolled 2-way `merge`+`encode`). EMPIRICAL BLOCKER: the plugin and runtime-wasm are two INDEPENDENTLY-linked Rust modules that both hard-code `__stack_pointer = 1048576` (1 MiB) + data above it — identical layout, 1:1 collision. A naive merge corrupts memory (plugin stack frames + .rodata clobber runtime's). The user/runtime pair only coexists because the Rayzor wasm_backend is HAND-COORDINATED: user data at low offsets (4096+, 428 active segments), shadow stack at ~16 MiB (global0=16773120). runtime-wasm uses passive segments + `__wasm_init_memory` start fn; the Rayzor backend uses active segments (auto-init). A merged 3rd module's passive segments also need ITS `__wasm_init_memory` run, but wasm allows only ONE start fn.\n\n**Two viable paths for 3/4 (pick next arc):**\n- **(a) PIC side-module** — build plugin relocatable (`__memory_base`/`__table_base` + GOT.func/GOT.mem imports); linker assigns memory_base above runtime's data + resolves GOT. This is how real wasm dynamic linking (Emscripten main/side module) avoids the collision BY DESIGN. BLOCKED at build today: `wasm32-wasip1-threads` force-injects `--export-memory`, and rust-lld rejects `--export-memory` with `-shared` (\"incompatible\"). Needs custom target spec / post-link flag stripping to drop `--export-memory`, THEN a GOT/`__memory_base` resolution pass added to wasm_linker. Principled but a real arc.\n- **(b) hand-coordinated layout** — build plugin with `--global-base=BASE` (BASE above runtime's data-end) + small `-z stack-size` into a RESERVED hole, AND rebuild runtime-wasm with `__heap_base` bumped above that hole so dlmalloc never hands out plugin-data addresses. Fragile (fixed reservation size; runtime's growing heap collides with any fixed high region otherwise) but no dynamic-linker pass needed.\n\n**PIC path CHOSEN (user, 2026-06-14) + build recipe DERIVED + VERIFIED to the libc wall.** `--experimental-pic` alone produces NO `__memory_base`/GOT (not actually relocatable). `-shared`/`-pie` are the real side/main-module flags but (i) conflict with the threads target's `--export-memory` (in `pre-link-args`, NOT reachable via `-C link-arg`) and (ii) fail because precompiled wasm std is non-PIC. Both solved by build-std + a stripped target spec. EXACT recipe (verified: std/core/alloc recompile PIC clean):\n1. `rustc -Z unstable-options --print target-spec-json --target wasm32-wasip1-threads \u003e spec.json`, then strip every arg containing `export-memory` from ALL `pre-link-args` arrays (2 entries: `--export-memory` for wasm-lld + `-Wl,--export-memory,` for wasm-lld-cc) → `wasm32-wasip1-threads-pic.json`.\n2. Build: `RUSTFLAGS=\"-C target-feature=+atomics,+bulk-memory,+mutable-globals,+simd128 -C relocation-model=pic -L $(rustc --print sysroot)/lib/rustlib/wasm32-wasip1-threads/lib/self-contained -C link-arg=--experimental-pic -C link-arg=-shared -C link-arg=--unresolved-symbols=import-dynamic\" cargo +nightly build --release --target ./wasm32-wasip1-threads-pic.json -Z build-std=std,panic_abort -Z json-target-spec` (the `-L self-contained` is REQUIRED — custom target name → rustc can't find `-lc` otherwise; `-Z json-target-spec` is REQUIRED for `.json` targets now).\n3. REMAINING BUILD WALL: the precompiled `libc.a` (wasi-libc) is non-PIC and ships as a binary archive (build-std doesn't rebuild it) → `R_WASM_MEMORY_ADDR_LEB cannot be used against symbol ...; recompile with -fPIC`. FIX = drop libc by making the plugin `no_std` on wasm. Feasibility CONFIRMED: nue-plugins' ONLY `std::` use is `std::arch::aarch64` (cfg'd out on wasm → scalar fallback); rayzor-plugin (dep, `plugin/`) uses only `Vec`/`Box`/`String`/`format!` (all in `alloc`) + a native-only `RuntimePluginRegistry` (unused on wasm). So: `#![cfg_attr(target_arch=\"wasm32\", no_std)]` + `extern crate alloc` on BOTH crates; wasm-only `#[global_allocator]` forwarding to the host-imported `malloc`/`free`; wasm-only `#[panic_handler]`; `core::arch::aarch64` instead of `std::arch`; `libm::expf`/`fabsf` for the `.exp()`/`.abs()` in nue-plugins/src/lib.rs (lines ~129, ~512); `half`/deps with `default-features=false`. Then -shared links clean → real side module (dylink.0 section, `__memory_base`/`__table_base` + `GOT.func.*`/`GOT.mem.*` imports).\n4. THEN add a GOT/`__memory_base` resolution pass to `wasm_linker.rs`: read the plugin's `dylink.0` (mem_size/mem_align/table_size/table_align), assign `__memory_base` above runtime's data-end (aligned) + `__table_base` above runtime's table, resolve `GOT.func.X`→merged func idx \u0026 `GOT.mem.Y`→assigned data address, append plugin internals/globals/data with its own `__wasm_init_memory` folded into a combined start. This is the genuinely new linker code (the 2-way merge has no GOT/relocation concept).\n\n**STEP 3a DONE (commit 7ab62e9, 2026-06-14): the PIC side-module is real + builds reproducibly.** `nue-plugins` + its `rayzor-plugin` ABI dep are now `#![cfg_attr(target_arch=\"wasm32\", no_std)]` (std kept on native), linking ONLY core+alloc (NO wasi-libc — that's what unblocked the -shared PIC link). nue-plugins has a wasm-only `#[global_allocator]` (HostAlloc → host malloc/free, shares the merged dlmalloc heap) + `#[panic_handler]` (wasm unreachable) + an `fmath` shim (libm expf/fabsf/roundf + inline clamp on wasm; inherent f32 methods on native). Host-only code gated to non-wasm so no core::fmt is dragged in: rayzor-plugin's RuntimePlugin/PluginRegistry, nue-plugins' plugin_init fn-ptr table. Cargo: `half` default-features=false (native-safe), `libm` as `[target.'cfg(target_arch=\"wasm32\")'.dependencies]`. Build recipe is `nue-plugins/build-wasm-pic.sh` (regenerates the export-memory-stripped threads target spec, `-Z build-std=std,panic_abort` + `relocation-model=pic` + `--experimental-pic -shared`; needs nightly + rust-src). Output `target/wasm32-wasip1-threads-pic/release/nue_plugins.wasm`. Native nue-plugins/rayzor-plugin build + clippy stay green. NOTE a linter twice tried to inject a duplicate `#[cfg]` — watch for that.\n\n**STEP 3b APPROACH DECIDED (user, 2026-06-14): STANDARD dynamic linking (dylink.0), NOT a bespoke static GOT-merge.** The PIC side-module 3a produced IS the standard Emscripten dynamic-linking format (dylink.0 + GOT). Rather than hand-writing a GOT-resolution pass into rayzor's static `wasm_linker.rs` (rejected as reinventing what standard wasm dynamic linkers do), do the merge as **runtime dynamic loading in the wasmtime embedder** (`compiler/src/codegen/wasm_runner.rs`): instantiate runtime-wasm (main) first, then load the side-module via `wasmtime::Linker` following the documented dynamic-linking ABI — read dylink.0 (mem_size/table_size), assign `__memory_base`/`__table_base` (Globals, above the main's data/table), wire the side-module's `env` imports (memory, malloc/free, rayzor_plugin_tensor_*, GOT.func.X→table idx, GOT.mem.Y→addr, __stack_pointer, __indirect_function_table) against the main instance, instantiate, then resolve the GUEST's `rayzor_kv_cache_q8_*` imports to the side-module's exports. CIRCULARITY (main's guest code imports Q8 fns; side imports main's malloc/tensor accessors) is broken the standard way: shared host-created memory + the main instantiates first (guest only CALLS Q8 fns at runtime, not at instantiate) with host trampolines for `rayzor_kv_cache_q8_*` that dispatch to the side instance (set via a OnceCell after side instantiation). REQUIRED 2-way-linker change: do NOT stub `rayzor_kv_cache_q8_*`/`flash_attn_decode_q8` — leave them as imports for the embedder's Linker to resolve. PACKAGING is the EXISTING rpkg (`rayzor rpkg pack --wasm \u003cside-module\u003e` → .rpkg; rpkg already has add_wasm_component/wrap_as_component) — do NOT add a new `rayzor plugin build` command (reverted, commit b49c447) and do NOT write a bash build wrapper (removed). The PIC build recipe lives in git history (`git show 7ab62e9:nue-plugins/build-wasm-pic.sh`) + this note; it gets a proper rpkg/runtime-driven home as part of this step.\n\n**STEP 3b SUPPORTS BOTH LOADING METHODS (user, 2026-06-14):** (1) **bundled** — side-module packed into an .rpkg (`rpkg pack --wasm`), runtime extracts + dynamically links it; (2) **on-demand dynamic load** — the browser win: async-fetch + instantiate the component ONLY when first needed (smaller initial download, lazy capability load). The host-trampoline design serves both: the guest's FIRST `rayzor_kv_cache_q8_*` call is the lazy-load trigger (sync side-module instantiate on native; async fetch+instantiate in the browser harness). Abstract the source as `SideModuleSource { DirectWasm(path|url), Rpkg(entry) }`; the dylink loader is source-agnostic.\n\n**STEP 3b EMBEDDER GROUNDING (read 2026-06-14, `compiler/src/codegen/wasm_runner.rs`, 4655 lines).** Instantiation core: `config.shared_memory(true)` + 4GiB reservation + `memory_may_move(false)` (≈1140); `Module::new(engine, merged_bytes)` (1152); `WasmState{shared_memory, tensor_handles, host_alloc_ptr…}` (1174); `Store::new` (1209); `Linker::new` + WASI p1 (1212); it then COLLECTS `module()==\"rayzor\"` func imports (1216-1228) and registers host funcs for them — THIS is where the Q8 trampolines slot in. SharedMemory is host-created (≈4592) + stashed on WasmState (wasmtime doesn't surface imported SharedMemory via caller exports — hence `read_wasm_mem`/`runtime_malloc` helpers at 459/637/653). Loader plan: (a) leave guest `rayzor_kv_cache_q8_*`/`flash_attn_decode_q8` as `\"rayzor\"` imports (2-way linker must NOT stub them); (b) register host trampolines for them on the Linker that, on first call, lazily `Module::new(side_bytes)` + instantiate in the SAME Store — wiring the side's env imports: `memory`→the shared memory, `__memory_base`/`__table_base`→host-created `Global`s (assigned above main's data/table per dylink.0 mem_size=49440/table_size=53), `malloc`/`free`→main's `rayzor_malloc`/`rayzor_free` exports (or the existing `runtime_malloc` host fn), `rayzor_plugin_tensor_*`→main instance's exports, GOT.func.X→table idx / GOT.mem.Y→__memory_base+offset, `__stack_pointer`/`__indirect_function_table`→main's, `memcmp`/`strlen`/`calloc`→host or main builtins; run the side's `__wasm_apply_data_relocs`/`__wasm_call_ctors` init; stash the side `Instance` in WasmState (OnceCell) — (c) the trampoline then dispatches the call to the side instance's export. This breaks the main↔side circularity (main instantiates first; side loads lazily on first Q8 call when main's exports already exist). Browser harness equivalent: same ABI in JS (async WebAssembly.instantiate of the side-module with the assigned base globals + main's exports as the import object). Phased: (1) native + DirectWasm + the trampoline/instantiate mechanics (validate e2e: RAYZOR_KV_Q8=1 wasm → coherent + sub-4GB); (2) Rpkg source; (3) browser async harness.\n\n**STEP 3b PHASE 1 — DONE \u0026 WORKING E2E (commits 6a6bfd1 loader, a9af36a/67c7967 fixes), 2026-06-14.** `RAYZOR_KV_Q8=1` on the wasm llama-chat now links the nue Q8 side-module via standard dylink.0 dynamic linking, engages the Q8 cache (`[kv-cache] Q8_0 mode enabled`, NO F32 fallback), coherent output, **peak RSS ~1.83GB @96 tok / ~2.39GB @320 tok vs F32 spiking to the 4GB wall + hanging** (Q8 cache preallocation for ctx=4096 is ~3.76× smaller: ~570MB vs ~2.1GB). Run it: `RAYZOR_KV_Q8=1 RAYZOR_Q8_SIDE_MODULE=\u003cabs path to side .wasm\u003e rayzor run --wasm \u003cMain.hx\u003e --no-cache -- \u003cmodel.gguf\u003e \"\u003cprompt\u003e\" \u003cntok\u003e` (the embedder's find_wasm_side_module looks under cwd `target/wasm32-wasip1-threads-pic/release/nue_plugins.wasm`; the env var overrides for any cwd). Native dylib (`target/release/libnue_plugins.dylib`) must exist for the manifest. **CRITICAL BUILD-RECIPE CHANGES (the deleted build-wasm-pic.sh recipe + these):** (1) build the side with `-Z build-std=core,alloc,panic_abort` — NOT `=std`: build-std=std links std's dlmalloc as a DEFINED `malloc` that SHADOWS the `env.malloc` import (the side then calls its own broken dlmalloc → null). (2) add `-C link-arg=--shared-memory -C link-arg=--max-memory=4294967296` (strip BOTH `export-memory` AND `max-memory` from the threads target spec) so the side's `env.memory` import declares max=65536 pages, matching the 4GiB host shared memory (else `incompatible import type for env::memory`). **THE killer bug (3hr hunt):** LLVM lowers the side's Q8-cache `malloc(n) + write_bytes(0)` into a single `calloc(1,n)` call; the embedder's `calloc` host shim was using `runtime_malloc(caller)` which resolves `rayzor_malloc` off the *caller's* exports — but the caller is the side module, which exports none → calloc returned null → cache handle null → silent F32 fallback (misleadingly logged as \"head_dim=64 not a multiple of 32\"). FIX: malloc AND calloc host shims call MAIN's `rayzor_malloc` directly (captured `instance.get_func(\"rayzor_malloc\")`), not `runtime_malloc()`. OTHER fixes: linker re-exports `rayzor_plugin_tensor_*` (RUNTIME_EXPORT_WHITELIST) so the embedder can `Instance::get_func` them for the side; read the side's table size + dylink.0 mem_size DYNAMICALLY (both shift across rebuilds — table 53→54, mem 49440→49456); `coerce_val` marshals guest Haxe-Int(i32) ↔ side i64 + narrows the i64 handle to the guest's i32 Ptr. Debug aids removed; gated `RAYZOR_Q8_TRACE` kept. **REMAINING for \u003c2GB on very long gens (user goal):** Q8 fixed the CACHE contributor but memory still climbs ~2.5MB/token = the per-token decode leak ([[bugs_decode_loop_per_step_leak]], ~8MB live/200k allocs per token) + the GGUF loader dequantises Q4_K_M weights to F32 at load (huge baseline). Wasm linear memory NEVER shrinks (no memory.shrink), so PEAK = RSS — must keep peak low, not just free(). Next levers: fix the decode leak; keep weights quantised in wasm (don't dequant to F32). Phase 2 = rpkg source; phase 3 = browser async lazy-load.\n\n**STEP 3b PHASE-1 IMPLEMENTATION PLAN — VERIFIED (workflow wa02eueze, wasmtime 46.0.0 source-checked).** Side-module surface: 52 imports (all module \"env\": memory[1..16384 shared], __indirect_function_table[funcref 53], __stack_pointer[mut i32], __memory_base[const i32], __table_base[const i32], malloc/calloc/free/strlen/memcmp, 6× rayzor_plugin_tensor_*, 24 GOT.func[mut i32], 12 GOT.mem[mut i32]). Exports: rayzor_kv_cache_q8_alloc/append (i64,i64,i64)-\u003ei64, dequant_view (i64,i64)-\u003ei64, free (i64)-\u003e(), flash_attn_decode_q8 (i64,i64,i64,i64,i64,f64)-\u003ei64, **__wasm_apply_data_relocs ()-\u003e()** (the ONLY init export — NO __wasm_call_ctors/__wasm_init_memory-as-export; __wasm_init_memory IS the Start fn, runs auto in Instance::new + memory.init's both passive data segs at __memory_base). dylink.0: mem_size=49440, mem_p2align=4, table_size=53. WIRING: env.memory→`store.data().shared_memory.clone()` (NOT get_memory — imported); __indirect_function_table→a NEW private `Table::new(funcref,53)` (runtime exports no table → __table_base=0, side owns it); __stack_pointer→NEW mut i32 Global = side stack top; __memory_base→NEW const i32 Global = MEM_BASE; __table_base→const 0; malloc→main_instance.get_func(\"rayzor_malloc\"), free→\"rayzor_free\" (sig match, name differs — positional Instance::new feeds by index so name-diff is fine); rayzor_plugin_tensor_*→main same-name exports; calloc/strlen/memcmp→host `Func::wrap` shims (no provider; calloc=malloc+zero, strlen/memcmp scan shared mem); GOT.func.X→Global(table_base+slot), GOT.mem.X→Global(mem_base+offset). **MUTABILITY must match import.ty()** (bases Const, GOT Var) else Instance::new bails. **Instance::new is POSITIONAL** — build the Vec by iterating `side_module.imports()` IN ORDER, matching (module,name); wrong order = silent base swap = corruption. **HARDENING (risk): place MEM_BASE by calling main's rayzor_malloc(49440-aligned + 64KiB stack) so the runtime allocator OWNS it — do NOT carve from host_alloc top (rayzor_malloc heap grows up unbounded, would stomp a host_alloc-carved block on a 770MB GGUF).** GOT resolution = STATIC parse of side bytes (wasmparser, already a dep): elem segment→func_idx→table slot; exported globals[40..53] const-init→data-symbol offset; map GOT.func.\u003csym\u003e→slot, GOT.mem.\u003csym\u003e→offset. Cross-check after instantiate: read side's re-exported globals, assert == computed (loud, not silent corruption). INIT after Instance::new: call __wasm_apply_data_relocs (guard if-let; patches GOT-relative ptrs into .data using the GOT globals — so GOT globals MUST be correct first). EMBEDDER POINTS: WasmState (wasm_runner.rs:22) gets `side_instance: Option\u003cwasmtime::Instance\u003e` (Copy, storeable); Q8 trampolines register `linker.func_new(\"rayzor\", name, ft, …)` near 1290 + add 5 names to `registered` so the FALLBACK STUB LOOP at **4558** (the return-0 closure — THIS is what currently neuters Q8) skips them; trampoline copies side_instance out of caller.data() (drops borrow), `side.get_func(\u0026mut caller, name)`, forwards params (already-typed I64/F64) via untyped `f.call(\u0026mut caller, \u0026args, \u0026mut out)`, sets results[0]. Side load block: between 4631 (after host_alloc seed) and 4633 (before _start). Gate the whole thing on Q8 imports present + side .wasm found (graceful: missing side → fall through to stub). Re-entrancy OK (side has own __stack_pointer; single guest thread). 12-step breakdown + full risk list in workflow wa02eueze output.\n\n**STEP 3b GOT/base RESOLUTION DETAIL (same numbers, now applied at instantiate-time in the embedder, not statically).** The side-module's `dylink.0` section gives **mem_size=49440, mem_p2align=4 (16-byte), table_size=53, table_p2align=0**. Its import surface (all module `\"env\"`):\n- `__memory_base` (global i32) → ASSIGN = runtime data-end aligned up to 16; the linker rewrites the side-module's GOT.mem against this. Reserve [base, base+49440) so runtime's dlmalloc heap (rayzor_malloc) never hands it out — i.e. the merged module's heap must start ABOVE base+mem_size.\n- `__table_base` (global i32) → ASSIGN = runtime table_size (append 53 slots); side-module funcrefs land at [base, base+53).\n- `__stack_pointer` (global) → wire to runtime's stack-pointer global (runtime global 0, mut i32; NOT exported by name — wire by index; SHARE it, the side module uses the host stack).\n- `__indirect_function_table` (table) → runtime's table (shared).\n- `memory` → shared memory (handled by existing merge).\n- `GOT.func.\u003cmangled\u003e` (~24, all core::fmt) → resolve each to the merged FUNCTION TABLE INDEX of that function (place the function in the table, emit the index as the GOT global's value).\n- `GOT.mem.\u003cmangled\u003e` (12, core unicode/flt2dec tables + task Waker VTABLEs) → resolve each to `__memory_base + the symbol's data offset`.\n- `malloc`/`free` → runtime `rayzor_malloc`/`rayzor_free` (reuse the existing alias table, wasm_linker.rs:607-637 — but note those match module==\"rayzor\" at :649; this side-module's are module==\"env\", so the new pass must alias env.malloc too, OR build the plugin with `#[link(wasm_import_module=\"rayzor\")]` on the extern blocks — UNVERIFIED whether PIC -shared honors that vs forcing env; test in the loop).\n- `rayzor_plugin_tensor_*` (6, the seam) → runtime exports these by the SAME name (8c6c473) → resolve by name.\n- `calloc`/`memcmp`/`strlen` → runtime-wasm does NOT export these. Provide via one of: (a) add `-Z build-std-features=compiler-builtins-mem` to the plugin build (defines memcmp/memcpy/memset/strlen IN the module, not imported) — try first; (b) export them from runtime-wasm (it has wasi-libc); (c) synthesize tiny linker stubs. calloc may need (b)/(c).\nThen: append the side-module's types/funcs/globals/data (data placed at __memory_base via an active segment or its own init), fold its `__wasm_init_memory` into a combined start with runtime's, and the guest's `rayzor_kv_cache_q8_*` imports resolve against the side-module's exports. Then 3-way link + e2e (RAYZOR_KV_Q8=1 on wasm → coherent + sub-4GB on a long gen). The mapping-workflow synthesis (task w64mwaeqk) has a fuller edit-level breakdown if needed.\n\n**After 3/4:** real `wasi_thread_spawn` in the embedder (`compiler/src/codegen/wasm_runner.rs` `run_pending_threads` currently runs guest threads INLINE/cooperative, not parallel) → `rayzor.concurrent` parallelizes IN-wasm → DELETE the host-sided matmul (b50b851). The plugin already exports `wasi_thread_start` (the thread entry), so the threads ABI surface is present.\n\n---\nArchived from Claude memory `project_wasm_q8_component` on 2026-10-18. A record of past work; open items in it may have their own issues.","files":null}]}